<div style="width: 100%; padding: 20px; background-color: #1a5276; color: #ffffff; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Historical Sites – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Historical Sites and Tourism</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Our Historical Sites</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Comprehend descriptive texts about places or events. Reading: Summarize the main points of different types of texts.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can comprehend descriptive texts about Indigenous events in Panama (e.g., "The festival has two main events: the traditional dance and the cooking contest. It's a time for the community to come together"). Can summarize the main points of a text about Indigenous history (e.g., "Identify customs practiced at the Ngäbe-Buglé community." "Describe the beadwork used in Emberá traditional clothing." "List the types of dances performed during Guna Yala ceremonies."). Can comprehend from descriptive texts details about Indigenous arts and crafts (e.g., "The Guna people use bright colors like red, yellow, and blue in their art.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: bold; color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of famous Panamanian historical sites (e.g., Casco Viejo, Panama Canal, Miraflores Locks, an Emberá village, Guna Yala islands). Students work in pairs to quickly name the sites and recall one fact or characteristic they remember from the previous lesson, using vocabulary like "historical sites," "heritage," or "attract."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher projects a short descriptive paragraph about a historical landmark like "Panama Viejo." The teacher reads the text aloud, modeling fluent reading and clear pronunciation. While reading, the teacher explicitly demonstrates how to identify the main idea and key descriptive details (e.g., underlining "ruins," circling "ancient city," highlighting "preserved"). The teacher emphasizes pronunciation of multisyllabic words like "historical," "cultural," and "information," and practices minimal pairs related to short/long vowels if they appear in the text (e.g., "sit"/"seat" if context allows).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher asks:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the main purpose of this short text? Is it to tell a story or describe a place?" (Expected: Describe a place.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What details help us imagine what Panama Viejo looks like now?" (Expected: Ruins, ancient, preserved.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0; text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: bold; color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Historical Sites and Tourism")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive a descriptive text titled "The Vibrant Heritage of Casco Viejo" (short article, informative register). The text describes Casco Viejo using present simple and simple past for its history and importance, future tense with "going to" for tourism plans, and possessive forms. It incorporates target vocabulary: <em>historical sites, Casco Viejo, architecture, heritage, destination, visitor, museum, tour, colony, port, attract, preserve, explore, tourist-friendly, vibrant, charming.</em></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading comprehension: Can understand general meaning and specific information in descriptive texts about historical sites and cultural heritage.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question (initial reading):</b> "What is the main topic of the text?" (e.g., a brief description of Casco Viejo as a historical landmark.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read statements about the text and mark them true or false (e.g., "Casco Viejo is an ancient ruin." "Visitors are going to find many museums there."). They must correct the false statements using information from the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match descriptions to specific parts of Casco Viejo mentioned in the text (e.g., "old churches" to "colonial architecture," "souvenirs" to "shops for visitors").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0; text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: bold; color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading descriptive texts, identifying main points, and summarizing. Grammar focus: Present simple/past simple for descriptions, "going to" for plans, possessive forms. Vocabulary: <em>historical sites, ruins, Casco Viejo, landmarks, heritage, monument, destination, visitor, museum, tour, attract, visit, explore, preserve, describe, informative, renowned.</em> Pronunciation focus: Stress in multisyllabic words (e.g., "cultural," "historical"). Pragmatic focus: Understanding intent (informative tone).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Cloze Text:</b> Students receive a paragraph about another Panamanian landmark (e.g., the Panama Canal) with gaps. They choose the correct vocabulary words from a box (e.g., <em>canal, transit, destination, attract, famous</em>) to complete the text. This reinforces vocabulary and reading for context.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Fact Sorting:</b> Students work individually to reread the text about Casco Viejo. They create two columns: "Historical Facts (Past Simple)" and "Current Attractions (Present Simple/Going to)". They underline and categorize sentences from the text into the correct column. For example, "It was once a vibrant colonial village" (Past Simple) and "Visitors are going to explore its narrow streets" (Going to).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – Identifying Key Details:</b> In pairs, students receive two short, different descriptive texts (e.g., Text A about a Ngäbe-Buglé traditional festival, Text B about Emberá beadwork). Each student reads their text and identifies 3-4 key descriptive details about the event, custom, or art. They underline the sentences containing these details. Then, they exchange texts and read their partner's highlighted details, discussing the tone of each article (e.g., "The tone of this article is informative because it describes facts about the festival.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0; text-align: center;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: bold; color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Historical Sites and Tourism")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Reading Summary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups of 3-4. Each group receives a unique descriptive text (e.g., "The Guna Yala's Mola Art and Ceremonies," "A Visit to the Ngäbe-Buglé Community Festival," "Emberá Artisans and Their Traditional Crafts"). These texts are slightly longer and more complex, detailing Indigenous events, history, or arts and crafts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Their task is to read the text collaboratively and then, as a group, identify and summarize the main points, specifically focusing on details like customs, beadwork, dances, or colors used in art, as per the learning outcomes.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups must write a short, concise summary (3-4 sentences) that captures the essence of their text and lists at least three specific descriptive details. They should explicitly use target vocabulary (e.g., <em>cultural, heritage, preserve, describe</em>) and grammar (present simple for descriptions, possessives).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One student from each group is going to read their summary aloud to another group, ensuring that they clearly articulate the stress in multisyllabic words and distinguish minimal pairs if present.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0; text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: bold; color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group work and summary presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students produce a written summary of their assigned text, which they then read aloud to a peer group.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can comprehend the main points of the descriptive text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify specific descriptive details about Indigenous events/arts/history.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Summarizes the text clearly and concisely (3-4 sentences).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Includes at least three target vocabulary words (e.g., <em>cultural, heritage, preserve</em>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Oral presentation): Uses intelligible pronunciation, especially stress in multisyllabic words.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket before leaving class.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most interesting fact you learned about an Indigenous historical site or tradition today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about reading and summarizing the text?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0; text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: bold; color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did identifying key vocabulary help you understand the texts better today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What reading strategy (e.g., underlining, summarizing) will you use next time you read a descriptive text?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The reading texts were appropriate for the A2.4 level, and students engaged well with identifying details and summarizing. The collaborative summary activity allowed for peer support. Some students struggled with differentiating between main ideas and supporting details when summarizing; I need to provide more explicit examples or sentence starters for summarization in future lessons. The focus on present simple and simple past for descriptions was well-received.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson built on the vocabulary from Lesson 1 and focused on comprehending those words in context through reading. The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will utilize these same texts and summaries as a basis for students to discuss, compare, and contrast historical sites and cultural practices, requiring them to actively use the learned vocabulary and grammar in spoken interaction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0; text-align: center;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>